# Combining conditions

One condition is often not enough. "Glucose below 100" alone would alert every
time you are sitting happily at 95. "Glucose below 100 **and** falling fast" is a
warning worth waking up for. This page covers the ways to combine conditions:
**all of**, **any of**, **NOT**, and groups.

The simulators on this page run Nocturne's real alert engine on made-up
readings. Under the graph, each condition gets its own line showing when it was
true; below those, **Whole rule is true** is the combined result, and
**Alerting** is when the alert was sounding.

## All of (AND)

With **all of** at the top of the condition, the rule is true only while
**every** row is true at the same moment. Each row is labelled **AND**.

Here are two rules on the same afternoon, where glucose falls fast during
exercise:

- **Low**: glucose below 70 mg/dL (3.9 mmol/L).
- **Falling toward low**: glucose below 100 mg/dL (5.5 mmol/L) **and** falling
  at least 2 mg/dL (0.11 mmol/L) a minute.

<AlertSimulator scenario="fast-fall" rules={["low", "falling-and-low"]} />

"Falling toward low" alerts ten minutes before "Low", because it notices the
speed as well as the number. Watch its two lights as it plays. "Below 100" stays
on right through the low, but "falling at least 2" goes out as the fall levels
off, and the rule stops with it. **All of** means the rule ends as soon as any
one of its rows stops being true.

Use **all of** to make a rule more specific: fewer alerts, each one more
meaningful.

## Any of (OR)

With **any of** at the top, the rule is true while **at least one** row is true.
Each row is labelled **OR**.

**Any of** makes a rule broader, and it is easy to make it too broad. Here is the
same pair of conditions with **any of** instead of **all of**:

<AlertSimulator scenario="fast-fall" rules={["falling-any"]} />

It alerts at the start of the fall, while glucose is still around 150, because
"falling at least 2" is true on its own, and then keeps going for the rest of the
afternoon because "below 100" is. Neither is what you would want to be woken up
for.

Use **any of** when different situations each deserve the same alert, as in the
next example.

## Groups

A group is a set of rows with its own **all of** or **any of**, sitting inside
the rule as a single row. Groups let you say things like "A, or both B and C".

To make one, open the **...** menu on a row and choose **Wrap in AND group** or
**Wrap in OR group**, then add more rows inside the group, or drag rows into it.

<Screenshot id="alert-rule-nested-group" callouts={[{ anchor: 'group', label: 'A group' }]} />

The rule in the picture alerts when glucose is below 70, **or** when it is both
below 100 **and** falling at least 2 mg/dL a minute. It catches a slow drift
into a low and a fast fall towards one:

<AlertSimulator
  scenario="fast-fall"
  scenarios={["fast-fall", "overnight-low"]}
  rules={["low", "low-or-falling"]}
/>

On the fast fall it alerts early and then keeps going through the low without a
break: as the "falling toward it" group stops being true, "below 70" has already
taken over. Switch to **Overnight low**, where glucose drifts down slowly: the
fall is never fast enough, so the group never becomes true, and the rule behaves
exactly like the plain low rule.

## NOT

**Wrap in NOT** turns a row into its opposite: the row is true whenever the
condition inside it is false. It shows a **NOT** badge.

NOT is most useful with time. "Glucose above 180 for at least 30 minutes,
**and NOT** between 18:00 and 20:00" is a high alert that stays quiet through
the rise you expect after dinner:

<AlertSimulator scenario="meal-high" rules={["high-outside-dinner"]} />

The light for the time condition shows the condition itself, the time being
between 18:00 and 20:00. NOT uses the opposite, so the rule can only be true
outside that window.

<Callout type="warning" title="NOT over missing data is true">

  Most conditions are simply not true when the data they need is missing. NOT
  turns that into true. "NOT pump suspended" is true on a site where the pump
  has never reported anything, and "NOT glucose below 70" is true while there is
  no reading at all. Check what NOT will do when data is missing before relying
  on it, and prefer the direct setting where there is one, such as "Pump
  suspended: is not" rather than "NOT pump suspended: is".

</Callout>

## Things worth knowing

- **A rule with one row is saved as that row.** The editor always shows a
  group, but a rule with a single condition is stored as just that condition.
  It behaves the same either way.
- **Every row is checked every time, in any order.** A row with "for at least"
  keeps its own clock whatever the rows around it are doing, so moving rows up
  or down never changes when a rule alerts.
- **Every condition in a rule sees the same moment.** The rule is checked when a
  reading arrives, and every row looks at that same reading.
